t -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}+\\int 8\\,{e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}-8{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-t}(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t)-16(1+t) \\\\ 2(1-2t)-8(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18t-15 \\\\ -12t-6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186167185 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186167185 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]
